---
description: Git conventions — when to commit/push/PR, renames, PR labels
alwaysApply: true
---

# Remote git (agents)

Unless the human **explicitly** asks, do **not**:

- `git commit`
- `git push` (or force-push)
- `gh pr create` / open or merge pull requests
- `gh pr merge` or other merge-to-default flows

**Commit alone is not permission to push.** Phrases like “commit”, “commit your changes”, or “commit if ready” mean **local commit only** — stop after `git commit` succeeds unless the same message also asks to push (e.g. “commit and push”, “push the branch”).

**Separate permissions:** push requires an explicit push (or ship) ask; opening a PR requires an explicit PR ask; merging requires an explicit merge ask (including round-trip / release skills that say GO). Updating PR title, body, or labels after a push is fine only when a prior instruction already covered that PR or the human asked to update it.

Working-tree **inspection** (`git status`, `git diff`, `git log`) is always fine. Do not stash, checkout, rebase, or rewrite history unless asked.

# Git renames

- **Renames and moves:** use `git mv <src> <dest>` for files and directories. Do not `mv` + `git add`/`git rm`, and do not use editor "Save As" + delete the old file.
- **Why:** `git mv` produces rename detection in diffs (`R` status), keeps `git log --follow` working, and makes PR review tractable during test restructures and refactors alike.
- **Same commit:** rename and update all import/require paths in one commit when possible.
- **Case-only renames (macOS):** two-step if needed — `git mv Foo.ts tmp.ts && git mv tmp.ts foo.ts` — so Git registers the case change.
- **Bulk renames:** still one `git mv` per path; verify with `git status` (should show `renamed:` not `deleted:` + `new file:`).

# Pull request labels

Use **exact** GitHub label names (including spaces after colons, e.g. `package: vm`). When opening or materially updating a PR, set labels via `gh pr edit`. **Do not** add unrelated labels (`prio:`, `eval:`, `effort:`, `good first issue`, `breaking`, `hardfork:`, `target:`, `dependencies`, `javascript`, `AI Usage`, …) unless a human explicitly asks.

## Package labels (`package: …`)

Apply **one or more** package labels for every package **substantially** changed in the PR (real behavior, APIs, benchmarks, tests, or package-scoped docs — not a drive-by one-liner in an unrelated package).

| Path / scope | Label |
| --- | --- |
| `packages/<name>/` (active library) | `package: <name>` — use the folder name (`statemanager`, `devp2p`, `binarytree`, …) |
| Root / cross-cutting repo infra | `package: monorepo` — e.g. `.github/`, `config/`, root `package.json`, `scripts/`, `.cursor/`, shared CI, monorepo docs |

Monorepo-wide PRs often need **`package: monorepo` plus** each substantially touched package. Pure single-package work uses only that package label. If the change is **only** repo infra with no meaningful package delta, `package: monorepo` alone is enough.

## PR state (`PR state: …`) — pick exactly one

Update this when the PR is created and again when intent changes (including after pushes that finish or reopen work).

| Label | When |
| --- | --- |
| `PR state: WIP` | More commits or known follow-up expected before review/merge; **Performance benchmarks** CI skips while this label is on the PR |
| `PR state: merge ready` | Author expects the PR to be complete and mergeable (default when handing off for review unless told otherwise) |
| `PR state: needs review` | Human explicitly asked for review attention (instead of or in addition to signaling readiness — use **instead of** `merge ready`, not both) |

Replace the previous PR state label when switching (remove the old `PR state: …` label). Do not set `PR state: needs changes`, `do-not-merge`, or `open discussion` unless a maintainer workflow requires it.

## Type labels (`type: …`) — non-test

Add **one or more** that describe the work (ordinary taxonomy, not CI triggers):

`type: bug`, `type: feature`, `type: enhancement`, `type: docs`, `type: refactor`, `type: tests`, `type: performance`, `type: security`, `type: CI`, `type: meta`, `type: maintenance`, `type: release`, `type: spec updates`, `type: external`, `type: discussion / question`

## Test-triggering and test-skipping types

These **change CI behavior** — add or remove deliberately; **when in doubt, prefer more testing** (do not add skip labels to save CI unless the human asked or the change clearly cannot affect that runner).

| Label | Effect (summary) |
| --- | --- |
| `type: test all hardforks` | Extra VM state/blockchain runs across hardforks (`vm-pr.yml`) |
| `type: test devp2p` | Runs devp2p build workflow |
| `type: test verkle` | Verkle-related test path |
| `type: test no browser` | Skips browser workflow |
| `type: test skip most stable VM` | Skips most stable VM jobs in `vm-pr.yml` |
| `type: test skip dev VM` | Skips dev VM EST jobs |

Remove a skip label when the PR no longer justifies skipping that coverage.

## Agent checklist

1. Infer packages from the diff; apply matching `package: …` (+ `package: monorepo` when appropriate).
2. Set one `PR state: …` label.
3. Set matching non-test `type: …` label(s).
4. Add or drop test-trigger/skip `type: test …` labels only when justified; default to **not** skipping heavy suites.
5. Leave all other label families unset.
